WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:02.020
Hi everyone, my name is Sai Vennam,

00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:03.699
and I'm with the IBM Cloud team.

00:00:03.699 --> 00:00:05.759
Today, we want to talk about container orchestration.

00:00:05.759 --> 00:00:10.240
I know that in the past, we've talked about containerization technology

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:14.460
- as well as dived into Kubernetes as an orchestration platform.

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.180
But, let's take a step back,

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:21.100
and talk about why container orchestration was necessary in the first place.

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:22.760
We'll start with an example.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:25.460
Let's say that we've got 3 different microservices

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:27.440
that have already been containerized.

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:30.060
We've got the frontend,

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:32.680
we'll have the backend,

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:36.660
as well as a database access service.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:39.180
These 3 services will be working together,

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:41.420
and are also exposed to end users,

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:44.220
so they can access that application.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:48.820
The developer has a very focused look at this layout.

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:51.840
So, they're thinking about the end user,

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:55.120
the end user accessing that frontend application,

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:58.540
that frontend, which relies on the backend,

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:02.700
which may, in turn, store things using the  database service.

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:06.580
The developer is focused entirely on this layer.

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:10.140
Underneath it, we've got an orchestration layer.

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:12.720
So, we can call that a master,

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.800
and I'm thinking about Kubernetes right now,

00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:18.220
where you would have something like a master node

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:20.940
that manages the various applications

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:23.300
running on your computer  resources.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:28.100
But, again, a developer has a very singular focused look at this layout

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:31.140
and they're really only looking at this stack right here.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:33.340
They're thinking about the specific containers

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:35.300
and what's happening within them.

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:38.160
Within those containers, there are a few key things.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.880
So, there's going to be the application itself,

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:44.120
there's also going to be things like the operating system,

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:46.660
as well as dependencies.

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:50.280
And there are going to be a number of other things that you define,

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:55.740
but all of those things are contained within those containers themselves.

00:01:55.740 --> 00:02:00.460
An operations team has a much larger view of the world.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:03.500
They're looking at the entire stack.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:05.940
So, an operations team:

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:08.640
there's a number of things that they need to focus on,

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:12.700
but we'll use this side to kind of explain how they work with

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:15.870
deploying an application that is made up of multiple services.

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:21.760
So, first, we'll talk about deploying.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:23.620
So, taking a look here,

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:26.060
it's very similar to over here, but the key difference is

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:27.600
these are no longer containers,

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:29.500
but the actual computing resources.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:31.660
This can be things like VMs (Virtual Machines)

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:35.480
or, in the Kubernetes world, we call these "worker nodes".

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:39.220
So, each one of these would be an actual

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:41.240
computing worker node.

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:43.000
So, you know, it could be something like

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.980
4 vCPUs (virtual CPUs) with 8 GB of RAM

00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:50.480
per each one of these different boxes that we have laid out here.

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:53.800
The first thing you would use an orchestration platform to do

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:57.240
is something simple - just deploying an application.

00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:59.900
Let's say that we start with a single node.

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:04.820
And, again, here we've got the master.

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:09.500
On that single node, we'll deploy 3 different microservices

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:10.720
- one instance each.

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:15.860
So, we'll start with the front end,

00:03:15.860 --> 00:03:20.020
we'll have the backend,

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:24.160
as well as the database access service.

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:26.060
Already, let's assume that

00:03:26.060 --> 00:03:29.430
we've consumed a good bit of the compute resources

00:03:29.430 --> 00:03:32.120
that are available on that worker node.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:36.000
So, we realize - let's add additional worker nodes to our master

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:40.410
and start scheduling out and scaling our application.

00:03:40.410 --> 00:03:46.520
So, that's the next piece of the puzzle.

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:48.860
The next thing an orchestration platform cares about

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:50.720
is scaling an application out.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:56.080
So, let's say that we want to scale out the frontend twice.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:01.840
The backend, we'll scale it out 3 times.

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:04.400
And the database access service,

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:09.060
let's say we scale this one out 3 times as well.

00:04:09.070 --> 00:04:13.800
An orchestration platform will schedule out our different

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:17.620
microservices and containers to make sure that

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:21.639
we utilize the computer resource in the best possible way.

00:04:21.639 --> 00:04:25.919
One of the key things that an orchestration platform does is scheduling.

00:04:25.919 --> 00:04:27.900
Next, we need to talk about

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:30.440
networking and how we enable

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:33.289
other people to access those  services.

00:04:33.289 --> 00:04:39.800
That's the third thing that we can do with an orchestration platform.

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:42.900
So, that includes creating things

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:47.360
like services that represent each of our individual containers.

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:49.900
The problem is:  without having

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:52.800
something like an orchestration platform take care of this for you

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:56.320
- you would have to create your own load balancers.

00:04:56.320 --> 00:04:59.360
In addition, you would have to manage your own services

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:02.380
and service discovery, as well.

00:05:02.389 --> 00:05:04.820
So, by that, basically I mean

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:07.300
that if these services need to talk to one another,

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:10.580
they're not going to try to find the IP addresses of each different container

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:12.660
and resolve those and see if they're running.

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:15.060
That's something the orchestration platform needs to do

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:17.319
- is handle that system around it.

00:05:17.319 --> 00:05:19.600
So, with this, we have the ability

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:23.140
to expose singular points of access

00:05:23.140 --> 00:05:25.940
for each of those services.

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:30.720
And again, very similarly, an end user

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:33.080
might access that frontend application

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:36.820
- so the orchestration platform would expose that service to the world,

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:39.160
while keeping these services internal

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:41.200
- where the frontend can access the backend,

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:44.300
and the backend can access that database.

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:46.040
Let's say that that's the third thing

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:49.560
that an orchestration platform will do for you.

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:56.720
The last thing I want to highlight here is insight.

00:05:56.720 --> 00:05:58.580
Insight is very important

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:01.460
when working with an application in production.

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:05.220
So, developers are focused on the applications themselves,

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:09.520
but let's say that one of these pods accidentally goes down.

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:12.180
What the orchestration platform will do

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:14.540
is it will rapidly bring up another one,

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:17.740
and bring it within the purview of that service.

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:19.800
It will do that for you automatically.

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:23.500
In addition, an orchestration platform has a number of pluggable points

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:25.780
where you can use key open source technologies

00:06:25.780 --> 00:06:28.120
- things like Prometheus and Istio

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:30.360
- to plug in directly into the platform

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:33.180
and expose capabilities that let you do things like a logging,

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:35.920
analytics, and there's even a cool one,

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:37.520
something that I want to sketch out here,

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:41.200
- the ability to see the entire service mesh.

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:43.400
Many times, you might want to

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:46.060
lay out all of the different microservices that you have

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:47.820
and see how they communicate with one another.

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:50.080
In this example, it's fairly straightforward,

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:52.760
but let's go through the exercise anyway.

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:54.860
So, we've got our end user;

00:06:54.860 --> 00:07:00.680
and the end user would likely be accessing the frontend application.

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:03.300
And, we've got the two other services as well:

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:09.040
the database, as well as the backend.

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:11.260
In this particular example, I'll admit,

00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:12.940
we have a very simple service mesh

00:07:12.940 --> 00:07:14.760
- we've only got three services.

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:18.260
But seeing at how they communicate with one another

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:20.000
can still be very valuable.

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:21.960
So, the user accesses the frontend,

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:23.640
the frontend accesses the backend,

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:26.100
and we expect the backend to access the database.

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:28.200
But, let's say the operations team finds that,

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:31.000
oh actually, sometimes the frontend

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:33.560
is directly accessing the database service.

00:07:33.560 --> 00:07:35.840
They can see how often, as well.

00:07:35.849 --> 00:07:37.080
With things like a service mesh,

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:40.160
you get insight into things like the operations per second.

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:41.920
Let's say that every time

00:07:41.920 --> 00:07:45.200
- or let's say there are 5 operations per second hitting the frontend,

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:47.980
maybe 8 that go to the backend,

00:07:47.980 --> 00:07:51.800
maybe 3 that go per second to the database service,

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:54.340
but then .5 requests per second

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:56.820
going from the frontend to the database service.

00:07:56.820 --> 00:08:00.720
The operations team has identified,

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:02.880
by taking a look at the requests

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:05.760
and tracing them through the different services,

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:07.900
that here's where the issue is.

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:09.500
This is a simple example

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:13.720
about how you can use something out like Istio and Kiali

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:16.140
(which is a key service-meshing capability)

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:19.680
to gain insight into running services.

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:23.380
Orchestration platforms have a number of capabilities

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:24.720
that they need to support,

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:26.860
and this why operations teams

00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:28.820
and these roles that we're seeing pop up

00:08:28.820 --> 00:08:31.620
- things like SREs (Site Reliability Engineers)

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:34.060
- and we're seeing the growth of those roles because

00:08:34.060 --> 00:08:38.860
there are a lot of things that they need to concern themselves with

00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:40.820
when running an application in production.

00:08:40.820 --> 00:08:43.320
Developers see a very singular view of the world,

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:46.180
where they're focusing on the things within the containers themselves.

00:08:46.180 --> 00:08:48.060
Thanks for joining me for this quick overview

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:50.040
of container orchestration technology.

00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:53.140
If you like this video please be sure to drop a comment below

00:08:53.140 --> 00:08:55.460
or leave us any feedback and we'll get back to you.

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:56.840
Be sure to subscribe,

00:08:56.840 --> 00:08:59.080
and stay tuned for more videos in the future.

00:08:59.080 --> 00:09:03.220
Thank you.

